\details{
A \code{Manager} runs one or more controllers in a single R process. Each
controller watches a primary resource (and optionally the resources it
creates), turns every change into a key on a \link{WorkQueue}, and calls your
\code{reconcile} function with that key. This is the same model as
controller-runtime in Go and kopf in Python, in one loop instead of
threads.

\code{reconcile(req)} or \code{reconcile(req, k)} receives \code{req}, a list with
\code{key}, \code{namespace} and \code{name}, and (if it takes a second argument) the
\link{Kube} session. It should read the current state, make the cluster match
what the object asks for, and return:
\itemize{
\item \code{NULL} (or anything else): done until the next change.
\item \code{\link[=requeue]{requeue()}}: try again soon, with per-key exponential backoff.
\item \code{\link[=requeue_after]{requeue_after()}}\code{(seconds)}: look again after a delay (e.g. to poll
something outside the cluster).
}

An error is logged with \code{\link[=message]{message()}} and retried with backoff; it does not
stop the manager.

By default reconciles run one at a time in the manager's process, so a
slow one delays the rest. With \code{workers = n}, up to \code{n} run at once in
background R processes. Each worker has its own \link{Kube} session, built
from the manager's connection settings. The reconcile function is sent
to the workers together with the functions and values it refers to from
the global environment (helpers defined in your script), but workers do
not share variables with the manager: assigning with \verb{<<-} inside a
reconcile changes the worker's copy only. Load any packages a reconcile
needs inside it or call them with \code{pkg::fn()}. Workers need rk8s
installed.

Changes to owned resources (\code{owns}) reconcile their owner: the object
named by an \code{ownerReferences} entry with \code{controller: true} whose kind
and API group match the primary resource.
}
\examples{
\dontrun{
k <- kube()
mgr <- Manager$new(k)
mgr$controller(
  "example.com/v1:Site",
  owns = "apps/v1:Deployment",
  reconcile = function(req, k) {
    site <- tryCatch(kube_get(k, "example.com/v1:Site", req$name,
                              namespace = req$namespace),
                     rk8s_api_error = function(e) NULL)
    if (is.null(site)) return(NULL)        # deleted; owned objects are GC'd
    # ... kube_apply() the Deployment it needs ...
    requeue_after(300)
  }
)
mgr$run(leader_election = "site-operator")
}
}
